WEBVTT

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:20.700
December 21st, 1968, the shortest day of the year, but in significance, perhaps one of the longest in the flow of history.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:29.970
This is Apollo Saturn launch control. We are still go at this time. T-minus 15, 14, 13.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:59.440
18, 12, 11, 10, 9, we have ignition sequence start, the engines are on, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0, we have liftoff, liftoff at 7.51 a.m. Eastern Standard Time, we have cleared the top.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:09.850
clear 13 seconds. The United States was undertaking the most distant voyage ever

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:16.290
attempted by man. For the first time three Americans rode the Saturn 5 moon rocket.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:12.960
Frank Borman, Jim Lovell, and Bill Anders were about to leave their cradle earth and face the infinite frontier.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.380
You are GO-PLI.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:39.780
TLI, Translunar Insertion. This was the commitment. Borman, Lovell, and Anders were ready for the

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:46.540
maneuver that would send them to the moon. As the world listened and watched, its people were

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:52.090
overtaken by a new awareness, an awareness that they were perhaps witnessing the overture to

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:53.910
the ultimate destiny of man.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.030
And the thrust is okay, Booster says.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:10.310
On board the spacecraft, and in mission control, the men of Apollo 8 watched the readouts.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.880
Velocity buildup in feet per second.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.080
The numbers snowballing toward the velocity that would allow the spacecraft to escape Earth's gravity.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.730
And Norman says we've got SECO.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:26.350
Was right on the second.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.500
cutoff of their third stage engine, Apollo 8 was traveling faster than had any men before,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:43.690
a coasting uphill climb against the pull of their native planet. Now the third stage, the S-4B,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:49.710
was just useless mass to them. It was jettisoned, then later placed into an orbit around the sun.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.060
The crew of Apollo 8 turned around for a look.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.100
The S-4B, the post-separation point is completed and we seem to have a high...

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.910
Give us a clue as to what it looks like from way up here.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:21.360
I can see the attack in Central America.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:28.160
America, in fact, all the way down through Argentina, down through...

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:31.660
Hey, you picked a good day for us.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:37.510
Good for information.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.710
Looking through the scanning telescope now,

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:45.890
and I see billions of stars, most of them need more B.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:51.610
The crew of Apollo 8 now settle down to the routine of the outward flight.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:56.700
Systems checks, observations, navigational star sightings.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:07.050
been looking at your marks with respect to accuracy, and they figure they're within a

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:09.090
thousandth of a degree of the...

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:16.100
Lovell's proficiency in navigating the spacecraft with its onboard optical instruments would

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:21.710
eventually earn him the nickname, the man with the golden fingers. His speed was such that

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:26.180
he would be requested to slow down so that the earthbound machines recording the data

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:29.980
could keep up with him. The accuracy of his sightings was very...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.340
virtually flawless, symbolic of the entire mission. A mission so accurate that several

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:43.100
of the planned mid-course corrections would be dropped as unnecessary. Astronauts, spacecraft,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:52.060
flight controllers, computers. The precision was fantastic. Then, on the second day out,

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.850
the world looked in on the crew via television.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:16.490
So Apollo 8 glided on silently, farther from Earth than man had ever before been,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.330
a microscopic dot of life in the cosmic void.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:27.880
Around the world, in Goldstone, California, Madrid, Spain, Canberra, Australia,

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:29.960
the gigantic antennas of the...

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.820
deep space network tracked Apollo 8, received its communiques, picked up the

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:39.500
voice of its telemetry. Only for the 40 minutes of its transit behind the moon

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:45.060
on each orbit would Apollo 8 lose contact with Earth. Then, on the day

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:50.150
before Christmas, the network zeroed in on the spacecraft television antenna for

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.810
the second broadcast to Earth.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:22.870
Shortly after they turned off the television camera,

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:27.320
Borman, Lovell, and Anders ended their long climb away from Earth,

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:29.970
crossing the point where the gravity of...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.540
Earth and Moon just balanced. They became the first men to fall toward another celestial body.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:41.730
Now the time approached for the most immense commitment of the mission,

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:50.070
Lunar Orbit Insertion, LOI. A burn of the service module engine would place Apollo 8 into orbit

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:57.490
around the Moon. Up to this time, without further major maneuvers, the spacecraft would loop around

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:59.150
the moon and return to earth.

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:06.540
Once the burn was made, Apollo 8 would be held by the Moon's gravity field until a later burn would push it free.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:10.200
This is Houston at 68-0.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:22.490
Lunar orbit insertion would take place on the backside of the Moon.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:29.160
With the Moon between the spacecraft and the Earth, all contact would be lost until it appeared on the other side.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:36.320
In Mission Control, they anticipated LOS, loss of signal, from Apollo 8.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:42.090
Now, Mission Control and the world could only wait.

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:47.110
Wait for the first contact with Apollo 8 as it emerged from behind the moon.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:52.620
We've got it. We've got it. Apollo 8 now in lunar orbit.

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:58.400
There's a chair in this room. This is Apollo Control Houston, switching now to the voice of Jim Lovell.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.660
The burn was as close to perfect as possible.

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:12.670
Later burns would circularize the orbit, and the three astronauts would circle the moon 10 times in 20 hours.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:18.760
Sunrise, sunset, every two hours on an alien world.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.340
And they would take pictures, still photographs as well as motion picture film,

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:28.920
here seen at a much faster rate than orbital velocity.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.220
Apollo 8, Houston. What is the old...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:23.360
Saw and heard the first live telecast of the lunar surface.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:59.540
Scientists, engineer, astronaut, the world, all followed the progress of Apollo 8, for this was preparation.

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:03.680
an advanced scout marking the way for those who would follow,

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:12.890
those who would orbit, those who would land, navigate, track, observe, record, describe.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:59.550
Then, a Christmas Eve message the world could not forget.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:29.530
Beyond technology, beyond science.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:37.360
Apollo 8 was bringing to the world a new awareness, an expansion of mind and spirit that must forever

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:58.820
alter the perspective of humanity, physically and philosophically. There was little time for the

00:12:58.820 --> 00:12:59.880
crew of Apollo 8.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.700
to reflect. The next job was to make sure that the spacecraft was ready for the most

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:11.280
critical maneuver of the mission, the firing of the service module engine for TEI, trans-Earth

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:17.770
insertion, and the trip home. There was no go, no-go decision on this one. It had to

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:29.290
go. For the final time, Apollo 8 passed behind the moon, out of contact with Earth for this

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:29.870
most critical

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:57.450
of all the burns. On the ground, men waited. Three very tired astronauts were on their way home,

00:13:58.570 --> 00:13:59.970
and with them rode an on.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:07.080
of engineers, scientists, technicians, programmers, flight controllers, all those whose dedicated

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:16.990
efforts were Apollo 8. After catching up on their rest, homeward bound, there was more

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:20.800
a mood of elation contrasted with the outbound loneliness.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:26.060
So Michael, you guys are up there, and I said, who's driving?

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:42.710
We were enjoying the holiday in the Christian world, Christmas, and we paused for a moment in our celebrations to watch three travelers heading home.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:58.380
As many of us sat down to our Christmas meal, Borman, Lovell, and Anders were enjoying theirs.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:23.470
As on the way out, there were periods of work.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:28.200
The navigational ability of the spacecraft and crew was proving phenomenal.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:29.970
And periods of work...

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.280
Arrest a single man on watch.

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:35.120
Well, Ken, that just leaves you or I. How about...

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.560
It must be on Christmas.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:15:59.700
Final television transmission.

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:09.040
97,000 miles out, farther than man had been on any previous mission, but now tantalizingly close to home.

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:27.380
I think I must have the feeling that the one on a ferry is going back.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.040
Every second brought the spacecraft closer to its fiery entry, the final plunge to the safety of Earth.

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:41.860
On the morning of December 27th, the word was given to Apollo 8.

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:48.380
Pyrotechnic devices that would separate the command and service modules prior to entry were armed.

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:53.930
Apollo 8, looking good.

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:05.580
On schedule, the command module jettisoned the service module to expose the main heat shield,

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:14.150
aligned itself to entry attitude, and slammed into the atmosphere at 25,000 miles per hour,

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:18.290
faster than man had ever traveled until Apollo 8.

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:38.890
A tracking aircraft took these films of the entry, the spacecraft seen as an incandescent fireball.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:48.370
With the temperature on the heat shield reaching 5,000 degrees, the communications were blacked out.

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:54.600
The men in mission control waited once more.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:05.520
Ken Mattingly just put in a call and just frankly labeled it a radio check.

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:08.080
He's gotten no responses yet.

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:15.660
Ken Mattingly.

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:24.410
It's in Jim Lovell.

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:34.020
The landing was right on target.

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:46.130
Later, Frank Borman would report passing over the recovery ship

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:49.730
as Apollo 8 drifted down toward the sea on its parachutes.

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.980
As Borman, Lovell, and Anders stepped onto the carrier deck,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.810
a huge and patriotic celebration broke loose in Mission Control,

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:09.660
a celebration echoed by the hundreds of thousands of men and women who were Apollo 8.

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:12.310
Again, it's Apollo Control here.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:14.770
I'm not sure how well our voice is getting out.

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:29.580
I have never seen the degree of this emotional in any previous mission, including Alan Shepard's.

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:34.380
I've seen rallies in locker rooms after championship games.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:37.720
I've seen happy politicians after elections,

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:41.700
but none of them do justice to the spirit pervading this room.

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:46.080
But the triumph of Apollo 8 was beyond the bounds of nationalism.

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:49.140
It was a fruition of the ages of humanity.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:52.630
The first Chinese setting off a gunpowder rocket.

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:54.610
The Englishman, Newton.

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:56.830
The German, Kepler.

00:19:57.660 --> 00:19:59.380
The Russian, Tsiolkovsky.

00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:02.130
The American Goddard.

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:10.310
One moment in history, the nations of Earth were united in the eternity of deep space.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:17.280
In Havana, in Washington, in London, in Moscow, in Tokyo.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:25.380
All of mankind joined three Americans orbiting 60 miles above a stark, lifeless world.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:32.720
And the earth was without form.

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:36.020
Void was upon the thing.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:04.310
It makes you realize you have...

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:31.740
as far as the coming of the day of university.
